WEBVTT

NOTE Silicon Valley C Players, Chapter 36: Sick, Well, Fit — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.570
Silicon Valley C Players. Chapter thirty-six.

2
00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:08.550
Sick, well, fit. How companies should use AI

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.987
An illustration, not a true story.

4
00:00:10.987 --> 00:00:14.240
Picture a made-up company that runs a billing system.

5
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.580
Every dashboard is green. The site is up, tickets close on time, last month reconciles.

6
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:25.411
By any reading, it is well. Then the board asks for AI in every team by the end of the quarter.

7
00:00:25.411 --> 00:00:27.252
An agent goes live on refunds.

8
00:00:27.252 --> 00:00:36.582
Nobody ever wrote down what a correct refund looks like, the one person who knew the edge cases is out that week, and the error counter starts to climb.

9
00:00:36.582 --> 00:00:38.669
Nothing was broken the day before.

10
00:00:38.669 --> 00:00:44.070
There was just no margin. That gap, between well and fit, is what this chapter is about.

11
00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:51.267
In October two thousand two, Greg Glassman, the founder of CrossFit, published an essay called What Is Fitness?

12
00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:59.915
Championing a fitness program without clearly defining what it is that the program delivers, he wrote, combines elements of fraud and farce.

13
00:00:59.915 --> 00:01:02.510
So he defined it, in a way you could test.

14
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:12.104
One of his observations was that nearly every measurable value of health can be placed on a continuum that ranges from sickness to wellness to fitness.

15
00:01:12.104 --> 00:01:22.817
His examples: a blood pressure of one sixty over ninety-five is pathological, one twenty over seventy is normal, and one oh five over fifty-five is consistent with an athlete.

16
00:01:22.817 --> 00:01:28.204
Body fat of forty percent is pathological, twenty percent is normal, ten percent is fit.

17
00:01:28.204 --> 00:01:31.020
Dozens of other measures line up the same way.

18
00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:37.795
Done right, he wrote, fitness provides a great margin of protection against the ravages of time and disease.

19
00:01:37.795 --> 00:01:39.877
It is and should be super-wellness.

20
00:01:39.877 --> 00:01:42.079
Wellness is not the end of the scale.

21
00:01:42.079 --> 00:01:43.090
It is the middle.

22
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.602
Most people aim for well: numbers in the normal range.

23
00:01:46.602 --> 00:01:53.510
That is a reasonable goal if the alternative is being sick, and the wrong goal if you still have something hard to do.

24
00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.618
I think companies do the same thing.

25
00:01:55.618 --> 00:01:57.433
Most companies strive for well.

26
00:01:57.433 --> 00:02:03.990
I strive for fit. And the hard thing most companies now have to do is make AI part of how they actually operate.

27
00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.232
Here is what I believe about that, and it frames everything below.

28
00:02:08.232 --> 00:02:16.253
I strongly believe that AI is even more powerful to those that actually understand the fundamentals of the area they are applying AI to.

29
00:02:16.253 --> 00:02:22.150
Orders of magnitude more than the person vibing their way into a discipline AI gives them access to.

30
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.706
That is my view, not a measurement.

31
00:02:24.706 --> 00:02:31.134
I am not mocking the vibing: it gets a person, or a company, into a field they could not enter before.

32
00:02:31.134 --> 00:02:35.420
It gets them to well. Fundamentals plus AI is what gets them to fit.

33
00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:38.737
Well is familiar. The site stays up most days.

34
00:02:38.737 --> 00:02:41.769
Tickets get closed. You can explain last month.

35
00:02:41.769 --> 00:02:45.640
It is better than sickness, and it is a thin place to stand.

36
00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.923
Sickness you may have seen too.

37
00:02:47.923 --> 00:02:51.601
A number nobody can defend. A cutover nobody can roll back.

38
00:02:51.601 --> 00:02:55.029
A process that only works if one person is in the room.

39
00:02:55.029 --> 00:02:57.710
An AI pilot that nobody can say is working.

40
00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:05.820
Fitness is a margin. When a vendor fails, a key person is out, or demand spikes, you still have room to move before you are in trouble.

41
00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:10.507
In a later lecture, Glassman added the observation this chapter turns on.

42
00:03:10.507 --> 00:03:19.419
If you are fit, you first have to become well before you can become sick: fitness is a hedge against sickness, with wellness as an intermediate value.

43
00:03:19.419 --> 00:03:23.400
A fit company is less likely to go from fine to crisis in one step.

44
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:29.460
It has to use up its margin first, and if it is reading its markers, it can watch that happen and act.

45
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:35.015
Chapter seventeen, Falling and Getting Up, is about getting back up after a failure.

46
00:03:35.015 --> 00:03:36.878
Margin is the room to do that.

47
00:03:36.878 --> 00:03:39.920
This is not a metaphor I am forcing onto a hobby.

48
00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:46.253
It is the best picture I have for what a company needs when the work ahead is hard and partly unknown.

49
00:03:46.253 --> 00:03:49.109
AI adoption is both. Well will not carry that.

50
00:03:49.109 --> 00:03:49.730
Fit might.

51
00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.417
Medicine has lab values. A company has to choose its own markers.

52
00:03:54.417 --> 00:03:57.954
Here are five, with readings for sick, well, and fit.

53
00:03:57.954 --> 00:04:01.691
The readings are illustrations, not industry benchmarks.

54
00:04:01.691 --> 00:04:04.227
Choose your own, before you need them.

55
00:04:04.227 --> 00:04:06.229
Recovering from a bad release.

56
00:04:06.229 --> 00:04:09.165
Sick: days, and nobody is sure what changed.

57
00:04:09.165 --> 00:04:11.500
Well: hours, with a known rollback.

58
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.770
Fit: minutes, with a rollback you have rehearsed.

59
00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:17.773
Who can run a core process. Sick: one person.

60
00:04:17.773 --> 00:04:21.910
Well: two people. Fit: several people, from a written runbook.

61
00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:24.267
Where the numbers come from.

62
00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:26.489
Sick: argued about in meetings.

63
00:04:26.489 --> 00:04:32.294
Well: agreed this month. Fit: defined, sourced, and reproducible by someone else.

64
00:04:32.294 --> 00:04:35.089
Cash. Sick: a surprise. Well: budgeted.

65
00:04:35.089 --> 00:04:39.031
Fit: visible every week, with a plan for a bad quarter.

66
00:04:39.031 --> 00:04:42.758
When an AI agent says done. Sick: you take its word.

67
00:04:42.758 --> 00:04:47.990
Well: someone spot-checks. Fit: it shows evidence someone else can check.

68
00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:49.955
Glassman also offered a test.

69
00:04:49.955 --> 00:04:59.369
If anything in your life has one of these metrics moving in a wrong direction, he said, I want you to entertain the possibility you are doing something profoundly wrong.

70
00:04:59.369 --> 00:05:02.544
Under his program, he said, the markers improve together.

71
00:05:02.544 --> 00:05:07.001
Companies are good at averaging away the one marker that is going the wrong way.

72
00:05:07.001 --> 00:05:11.903
The agents ship more every week, so nobody looks at how much of it a person had to redo.

73
00:05:11.903 --> 00:05:13.908
Sometimes that is a trade you chose.

74
00:05:13.908 --> 00:05:18.030
Often nobody chose it. Find the divergent marker and investigate it first.

75
00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:20.615
CrossFit does not stop at the line.

76
00:05:20.615 --> 00:05:31.741
Its Level One Training Guide uses four models at once so that fitness stays broad: ten general physical skills, the hopper, three metabolic pathways, and the continuum itself.

77
00:05:31.741 --> 00:05:36.636
The aim is to prepare people not only for the unknown but for the unknowable.

78
00:05:36.636 --> 00:05:40.832
The summary is five words long: our specialty is not specializing.

79
00:05:40.832 --> 00:05:44.456
Not specializing is a claim about capacity, not strategy.

80
00:05:44.456 --> 00:05:48.080
Specialize in what you sell, not in what you can survive.

81
00:05:48.430 --> 00:06:00.584
The first model is a list of ten: cardiovascular and respiratory endurance, stamina, strength, flexibility, power, speed, coordination, agility, balance, and accuracy.

82
00:06:00.584 --> 00:06:06.625
The test is blunt: You are as fit as you are competent in each of these ten skills.

83
00:06:06.625 --> 00:06:10.264
A company does not need ten separate translations.

84
00:06:10.264 --> 00:06:11.720
Four groups will do.

85
00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:18.643
Endurance and stamina are sustained load: can you repeat hard efforts without the quality dropping by week nine?

86
00:06:18.643 --> 00:06:25.920
Strength, power, and speed are force: can you move something heavy, and shorten the cycle of the work you repeat every week?

87
00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:36.633
Flexibility, agility, and coordination are range: a quick change of pattern when the plan changes, and functions that combine into one movement instead of separate drills.

88
00:06:36.633 --> 00:06:43.542
Balance and accuracy are control: staying over your support base, especially on cash, and landing where you aimed.

89
00:06:43.542 --> 00:06:48.330
If a report column is named one thing and counts another, you are not accurate.

90
00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:51.398
The guide adds a distinction worth keeping.

91
00:06:51.398 --> 00:06:55.127
Some of the ten come from training, which changes the body.

92
00:06:55.127 --> 00:06:59.172
Coordination, agility, balance, and accuracy come from practice.

93
00:06:59.172 --> 00:07:05.176
A company builds some capacity the slow way, by changing its body: hiring, systems, AI tooling.

94
00:07:05.176 --> 00:07:08.147
Coordination and accuracy do not come that way.

95
00:07:08.147 --> 00:07:11.370
They come from doing the work together, many times.

96
00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.109
The second model is a thought experiment.

97
00:07:14.109 --> 00:07:18.188
Load a lottery hopper with as many physical tasks as you can think of.

98
00:07:18.188 --> 00:07:22.616
Line everyone up, turn the handle, pull a task out at random, and test them.

99
00:07:22.616 --> 00:07:26.170
Whoever performs best across the random draws is the fittest.

100
00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:34.019
Two things follow. First, Glassman said, the fittest person on earth may be only in the seventy-fifth percentile at each event.

101
00:07:34.019 --> 00:07:37.149
Fit means good at everything before best at anything.

102
00:07:37.149 --> 00:07:41.814
Second, everyone knows the task they do not want to see come out of the hopper.

103
00:07:41.814 --> 00:07:49.490
That task is a chink in your armor, and there is more to gain from going after it than from more time on what you already do well.

104
00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:54.618
Every company has a hopper. A payment processor goes down on your busiest day.

105
00:07:54.618 --> 00:07:56.880
Your most important engineer resigns.

106
00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:00.915
A model or a vendor changes its behavior or its pricing overnight.

107
00:08:00.915 --> 00:08:04.950
An agent confidently reports done on something it could not reach.

108
00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:09.840
You will not get the list in advance, but you already know which draw you dread.

109
00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:14.670
Practice that one: a tabletop exercise, a game day, a restore you actually run.

110
00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:18.530
The seventy-fifth percentile matters to whoever pays the bills.

111
00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:22.542
Margin is not free: a second person who can run payroll, cash held back.

112
00:08:22.542 --> 00:08:25.551
The model does not ask you to be the best at anything.

113
00:08:25.551 --> 00:08:28.560
It asks you not to have an event you cannot do at all.

114
00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:34.351
The third model is the three metabolic pathways that supply the energy for all human action.

115
00:08:34.351 --> 00:08:39.378
One dominates the highest-powered efforts, those lasting less than about ten seconds.

116
00:08:39.378 --> 00:08:43.458
One dominates moderate-powered efforts lasting up to several minutes.

117
00:08:43.458 --> 00:08:46.474
One dominates low-powered efforts that last longer.

118
00:08:46.474 --> 00:08:48.840
The guide wants competency in all three.

119
00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:51.499
A company runs on three engines too.

120
00:08:51.499 --> 00:08:54.833
The hours engine handles an outage or a bad release.

121
00:08:54.833 --> 00:08:58.040
The weeks engine is a hard push to ship by a date.

122
00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:03.619
The years engine is a migration, or the slow work of making AI part of how you operate.

123
00:09:03.619 --> 00:09:11.764
Many companies favor one: great in a crisis and adrift in between, or good at long programs and frozen when the pager goes off.

124
00:09:11.764 --> 00:09:12.790
Train all three.

125
00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:19.447
The models led to a one-line definition of fitness: work capacity across broad time and modal domains.

126
00:09:19.447 --> 00:09:21.179
Plot power against duration.

127
00:09:21.179 --> 00:09:25.075
Fitness is the area under that curve, not one high point on it.

128
00:09:25.075 --> 00:09:30.640
Excellent at a planned launch and weak when something unknown shows up is narrow capacity.

129
00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.438
Then Glassman added a third axis: age.

130
00:09:33.438 --> 00:09:37.691
Extend the area under the curve across a life and you get a solid.

131
00:09:37.691 --> 00:09:44.650
That is health. For a company, the third axis is years: through a bad market, a migration, a new technology.

132
00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:48.967
A company that has one great year and then falls apart was not fit.

133
00:09:48.967 --> 00:09:50.320
It had a good season.

134
00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:57.258
The prescription that builds this capacity is short: constantly varied, high-intensity functional movement.

135
00:09:57.258 --> 00:10:01.389
Functional movements move large loads over long distances, quickly.

136
00:10:01.389 --> 00:10:04.596
Intensity is defined as power: work divided by time.

137
00:10:04.596 --> 00:10:11.810
The variation is there because preparing for unknown events is at odds with fixed, predictable, and routine regimens.

138
00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:21.640
At work, functional means the movements that actually move the business: orders flowing, customers getting what they need, decisions landing, cash used on purpose.

139
00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:26.176
A standing meeting that never changes a decision is not a functional movement.

140
00:10:26.176 --> 00:10:29.142
Neither is an AI demo that never touches real work.

141
00:10:29.142 --> 00:10:31.177
High intensity does not mean panic.

142
00:10:31.177 --> 00:10:33.620
It means more real work finished per week.

143
00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:40.547
Every regimen, every routine, Glassman wrote, contains within its structure a blueprint for its deficiency.

144
00:10:40.547 --> 00:10:45.947
Every operating rhythm hides a blind spot: the thing the weekly review never asks about.

145
00:10:45.947 --> 00:10:48.463
Keep the calendar and vary the questions.

146
00:10:48.463 --> 00:10:56.687
Rest is in the program too. The guide's general pattern is three days on and one day off, chosen for sustainability at high intensity.

147
00:10:56.687 --> 00:11:00.553
Recovery is part of the program, not a reward for finishing it.

148
00:11:00.553 --> 00:11:03.990
At work, that is slack you plan, not slack you hope for.

149
00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:10.872
There is a warning attached to intensity, and it is the one I want on the table before anyone hears go faster.

150
00:11:10.872 --> 00:11:18.248
CrossFit's charter for balancing safety, efficacy, and efficiency is mechanics, consistency, then, and only then, intensity.

151
00:11:18.248 --> 00:11:23.660
Ignoring the order increases the risk for injury and potentially blunts long-term progress.

152
00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:27.071
This is where my belief about fundamentals pays off.

153
00:11:27.071 --> 00:11:34.842
AI is intensity. It amplifies whatever mechanics are already there, good or bad, and intensity on bad mechanics is how you get hurt.

154
00:11:34.842 --> 00:11:37.550
Intensity is also where the results come from.

155
00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:45.712
The person who knows the area can say what done means and check the work, so they can hand over more of it, with less watching.

156
00:11:45.712 --> 00:11:50.572
The person vibing cannot check, so they stay at low intensity or go fast blind.

157
00:11:50.572 --> 00:11:55.431
In my view that gap, not the tool, is where the difference in power comes from.

158
00:11:55.431 --> 00:12:05.150
So this is the order for rolling AI out across a company, and it is easy to run backward: a usage target for everyone, before anyone has said what done means.

159
00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:14.703
Mechanics first. Before an agent does a job, you can say what it can reach, what done means for this task, and which number you are actually asking for.

160
00:12:14.703 --> 00:12:22.090
Two of the AI Tips on this site are about exactly this: Know What the Bot Can Actually Reach, and Ask for Proof, Not Done.

161
00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:30.183
Then consistency. The same task, repeated, passing the same check each time, without a hero in the room to rescue it.

162
00:12:30.183 --> 00:12:35.146
Only then intensity: more tasks, more teams, more volume, less supervision.

163
00:12:35.146 --> 00:12:38.720
Skip the order and you get a good demo and a bad week.

164
00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:44.272
The guide also draws a pyramid, a theoretical hierarchy of development for an athlete.

165
00:12:44.272 --> 00:12:51.121
From the bottom: nutrition, metabolic conditioning, gymnastics, weightlifting and throwing, and sport at the top.

166
00:12:51.121 --> 00:12:55.242
Then: We do not deliberately order these components but nature will.

167
00:12:55.242 --> 00:13:00.454
If you have a deficiency at any level of the pyramid the components above will suffer.

168
00:13:00.454 --> 00:13:02.030
Here is a company version.

169
00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:04.178
Nutrition is what you run on.

170
00:13:04.178 --> 00:13:10.962
Cash you can see and steer, data with definitions, and access that matches the job, for people and for agents.

171
00:13:10.962 --> 00:13:18.238
Software Engineering 201 on this site has a lesson on agents that operate systems with the least access the job needs.

172
00:13:18.238 --> 00:13:21.137
Metabolic conditioning is the operating rhythm.

173
00:13:21.137 --> 00:13:23.850
The daily and weekly capacity to keep going.

174
00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:26.619
Gymnastics is control of your own body.

175
00:13:26.619 --> 00:13:32.139
Deploy, roll back, restore, and change your own process without waiting for outside help.

176
00:13:32.139 --> 00:13:35.053
Weightlifting is control of an external object.

177
00:13:35.053 --> 00:13:39.023
Vendors, partners, models, and the loads you pick up on purpose.

178
00:13:39.023 --> 00:13:42.930
Sport is the application. Out in the market, under competition.

179
00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:47.863
AI sits near the top of that pyramid, with the other loads you pick up on purpose.

180
00:13:47.863 --> 00:13:49.483
It does not replace the base.

181
00:13:49.483 --> 00:13:58.649
The guide says the model is most useful for analyzing an athlete's shortcomings, and that is how to use it here: when something at the top keeps failing, look down.

182
00:13:58.649 --> 00:14:05.970
An AI program that keeps disappointing often has a data problem, an access problem, or nobody has defined what finished looks like.

183
00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:13.571
CrossFit borrows one more word from gymnastics: virtuosity, defined there as performing the common uncommonly well.

184
00:14:13.571 --> 00:14:19.128
In a two thousand five open letter to trainers, Glassman described what gets in its way.

185
00:14:19.128 --> 00:14:24.179
Novices in any art hurry past the fundamentals toward more elaborate techniques.

186
00:14:24.179 --> 00:14:27.020
He called that compulsion the novice's curse.

187
00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:32.640
Rushing ahead, he wrote, increases the chance of injury, delays advancement and progress.

188
00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:41.668
Companies have their own version: the new tool, the new framework, the fleet of agents, adopted by a team that still cannot ship a clean release.

189
00:14:41.668 --> 00:14:46.330
Chapter six, Fundamentals Nobody Uses, is about principles that outlive tools.

190
00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:51.890
Chapter twenty-nine, The Ladder, treats AI coding as one more rung, from first principles up.

191
00:14:51.890 --> 00:14:54.460
A rung is no use without the ones under it.

192
00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:57.420
Virtuosity is also what makes AI safe to use.

193
00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:02.699
The person who knows the fundamentals can tell when the AI's output is wrong, and steer it.

194
00:15:02.699 --> 00:15:11.400
A company that does the common things uncommonly well, a clean handoff, a number with a definition, a release it can roll back, is hard to knock over.

195
00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:14.842
CrossFit calls its approach evidence-based fitness.

196
00:15:14.842 --> 00:15:23.511
Meaningful statements about safety, efficacy, and efficiency, the guide says, can be supported only by measurable, observable, repeatable data.

197
00:15:23.511 --> 00:15:25.511
A score on the whiteboard counts.

198
00:15:25.511 --> 00:15:27.754
A feeling after the workout does not.

199
00:15:27.754 --> 00:15:32.361
Hold your own work to that. A bot that says done has not given you a result.

200
00:15:32.361 --> 00:15:33.210
Ask for proof.

201
00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:43.171
In a twenty twenty-four talk, Glassman put the standard in one line: a model is a forecast of a measurement, and validation comes from predictive strength, period.

202
00:15:43.171 --> 00:15:46.001
A strategy is a model. So is an AI rollout plan.

203
00:15:46.001 --> 00:15:51.838
Write down the measurement you expect before you start, and grade the plan by whether it came true.

204
00:15:51.838 --> 00:15:55.080
Over several plans, you learn whose forecasts to trust.

205
00:15:55.080 --> 00:16:00.210
Trust works the same way: all rational trust, he said, is based on predictive strength.

206
00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:02.411
Numbers have their own failure.

207
00:16:02.411 --> 00:16:09.158
Goodhart's law, in Marilyn Strathern's phrasing: When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.

208
00:16:09.158 --> 00:16:12.383
Train for one score and the rest of the curve suffers.

209
00:16:12.383 --> 00:16:15.010
Several markers are harder to game than one.

210
00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:19.930
CrossFit measures progress with benchmark workouts, which hold the work constant.

211
00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:25.515
Fran is one: twenty-one, fifteen, and nine repetitions of ninety-five-pound thrusters and pull-ups.

212
00:16:25.515 --> 00:16:28.054
Every time you do Fran, the work is the same.

213
00:16:28.054 --> 00:16:33.921
So if you do it again a year later and finish faster, the difference in time is the difference in power.

214
00:16:33.921 --> 00:16:43.230
When Glassman introduced the benchmarks in two thousand three, he had them return through repeated, irregular, appearances, and asked everyone to post their results.

215
00:16:43.580 --> 00:16:46.150
A company can keep benchmarks the same way.

216
00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:52.009
Here is a made-up subscription-billing company, with one made-up pair of times, to show the shape.

217
00:16:52.009 --> 00:16:55.834
Restore yesterday's ledger from backup into a clean environment.

218
00:16:55.834 --> 00:16:57.986
In the spring it took most of a day.

219
00:16:57.986 --> 00:17:05.040
By fall, under an hour. Ship a one-line change from an approved ticket to production, with tests and a rollback ready.

220
00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:14.485
Have an agent reconcile one saved day of charges against the processor's report, and check its answer against the processor's totals, not its own summary.

221
00:17:14.485 --> 00:17:17.793
Close the month with numbers someone else can reproduce.

222
00:17:17.793 --> 00:17:22.163
Time them every quarter, unannounced where you can, and publish the times.

223
00:17:22.163 --> 00:17:27.183
The comparison holds only while the work really is the same, so write the drill down.

224
00:17:27.183 --> 00:17:29.900
A benchmark nobody has run lately is a memory.

225
00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:37.031
The guide makes a claim small teams should hear: Your needs and the Olympic athlete's differ by degree not kind.

226
00:17:37.031 --> 00:17:45.930
The principle for scaling a workout is to preserve the stimulus: adjust load, speed, and volume so the workout has a similar effect on each person.

227
00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:50.703
A five-person company does not need an enterprise disaster-recovery program.

228
00:17:50.703 --> 00:17:54.370
It needs to restore its database once, on purpose, and time it.

229
00:17:54.370 --> 00:17:56.697
It does not need an AI governance board.

230
00:17:56.697 --> 00:17:59.200
It needs to know what each agent can reach.

231
00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:01.427
None of this guarantees anything.

232
00:18:01.427 --> 00:18:07.341
In lectures given in twenty sixteen, Glassman put the outcome as a sum: fitness plus luck equals health.

233
00:18:07.341 --> 00:18:10.981
By luck he meant accidents, the things you can do nothing about.

234
00:18:10.981 --> 00:18:16.099
The outcome is the part you can do something about plus the part you can do nothing about.

235
00:18:16.099 --> 00:18:20.193
You cannot train luck away, and a hard enough hit can end a fit company.

236
00:18:20.193 --> 00:18:25.880
What fitness changes is the odds: more of the bad draws cost you some margin instead of the company.

237
00:18:26.230 --> 00:18:29.898
The last piece is how CrossFit gets people to work this hard.

238
00:18:29.898 --> 00:18:35.371
In implementation, the guide says, CrossFit is quite simply, a sport: the Sport of Fitness.

239
00:18:35.371 --> 00:18:41.265
The camaraderie and competition of sport yield an intensity that cannot be matched by other means.

240
00:18:41.265 --> 00:18:48.000
So it uses whiteboards as scoreboards, keeps accurate scores, runs a clock, and defines the standards precisely.

241
00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:50.032
It is also built in the open.

242
00:18:50.032 --> 00:18:58.851
We are an open-source fitness program, Glassman wrote in two thousand five, in an article that lists the innovations that came directly from our clients.

243
00:18:58.851 --> 00:19:01.387
And the gyms are affiliates, not franchises.

244
00:19:01.387 --> 00:19:09.918
A business profile in the CrossFit Journal quoted him on why: if I try to get everyone moving in lockstep, I get everyone moving towards mediocrity.

245
00:19:09.918 --> 00:19:13.780
Left to vary, he said, best practices arise at a breathtaking rate.

246
00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:20.932
Half of that is a sound way to spread AI through a company: the use cases come from the teams, and from customers.

247
00:19:20.932 --> 00:19:27.256
The other half a company has to add from the center: shared standards, open scores, and common guardrails.

248
00:19:27.256 --> 00:19:31.671
Score the benchmarks, not the usage, or the scoreboard becomes the target.

249
00:19:31.671 --> 00:19:37.100
And train together. Fitness in one department and sickness in another is not a fit company.

250
00:19:37.450 --> 00:19:40.273
I am not asking anyone to join a CrossFit class.

251
00:19:40.273 --> 00:19:42.920
I am asking you not to confuse well with fit.

252
00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:47.155
Well is a normal reading. Fit is a margin for when something goes wrong.

253
00:19:47.155 --> 00:19:52.449
If you train only to be well, you spend the hard days finding out how little room you had.

254
00:19:52.449 --> 00:19:55.860
If you train to be fit, you have already built the buffer.

255
00:19:56.220 --> 00:19:59.740
To place your team on the line, do not start with a feeling.

256
00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:02.204
Pick two or three markers you can observe.

257
00:20:02.204 --> 00:20:05.548
Would someone else get the same number from your sources?

258
00:20:05.548 --> 00:20:09.009
Can the core work run for a week if its usual owner is out?

259
00:20:09.009 --> 00:20:13.644
If an AI tool or a vendor disappeared tomorrow, how long before the work stops?

260
00:20:13.644 --> 00:20:19.393
If the same team did twice the volume next month, with AI doing the extra, what would break first?

261
00:20:19.393 --> 00:20:23.030
Which marker is moving the wrong way while the others improve?

262
00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:27.430
Which draw from the hopper do you dread, and when did you last practice it?

263
00:20:27.770 --> 00:20:32.204
Sick: you cannot answer, or the answer is: only if this one person is here.

264
00:20:32.204 --> 00:20:35.102
Well: you can answer, and the week usually works.

265
00:20:35.102 --> 00:20:41.310
Fit: you can lose a person, a vendor, or a week of luck and still deliver, and you can show the evidence.

266
00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:53.110
Glassman's two thousand two essay carried a box that put his whole method into a hundred words: Eat meat and vegetables, nuts and seeds, some fruit, little starch and no sugar.

267
00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:57.403
Keep intake to levels that will support exercise but not body fat.

268
00:20:57.403 --> 00:21:03.388
Practice and train major lifts: Deadlift, clean, squat, presses, clean and jerk, and snatch.

269
00:21:03.388 --> 00:21:13.147
Similarly, master the basics of gymnastics: pull-ups, dips, rope climb, push-ups, sit-ups, presses to handstand, pirouettes, flips, splits, and holds.

270
00:21:13.147 --> 00:21:16.204
Bike, run, swim, row, et cetera, hard and fast.

271
00:21:16.204 --> 00:21:23.165
Five or six days per week mix these elements in as many combinations and patterns as creativity will allow.

272
00:21:23.165 --> 00:21:26.678
Routine is the enemy. Keep workouts short and intense.

273
00:21:26.678 --> 00:21:29.020
Regularly learn and play new sports.

274
00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:32.056
A company's method should fit on one page too.

275
00:21:32.056 --> 00:21:37.251
Our adaptation is called Business Fitness in one hundred Words, and it closes this video.

276
00:21:37.251 --> 00:21:40.521
Try it with your AI Two prompts are on the chapter page.

277
00:21:40.521 --> 00:21:46.651
One places your team on the sickness, wellness, fitness line, and proposes three markers you can observe.

278
00:21:46.651 --> 00:21:52.255
The other scores your company against Business Fitness in one hundred Words, one line at a time.

279
00:21:52.255 --> 00:21:55.700
Both tell the AI not to invent facts about you, but to ask.

280
00:21:56.060 --> 00:22:00.296
The rule. Well is a reading and fit is a margin, so train for fit.

281
00:22:00.296 --> 00:22:06.650
Choose markers you can observe, time the same work every quarter, and practice the event you dread.

282
00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:12.940
Put AI on fundamentals you understand: mechanics first, then consistency, and only then intensity.

283
00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:19.317
In full: when you report how a team, a system, or an agent's work is doing, do not start with a feeling.

284
00:22:19.317 --> 00:22:25.238
Name the markers, give each a reading of sick, well, or fit, and show evidence someone else can check.

285
00:22:25.238 --> 00:22:31.332
If one marker is moving the wrong way while the others improve, say that first, and investigate it first.

286
00:22:31.332 --> 00:22:37.311
Before you start a plan, write down the measurement you expect, and afterward say whether it came true.

287
00:22:37.311 --> 00:22:43.290
Keep a short list of benchmark work, hold the work the same, time it every quarter, and post the times.

288
00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:46.811
Name the event you least want to draw, and rehearse it.

289
00:22:46.811 --> 00:22:59.378
Turn AI, scope, or speed up only where three things hold: someone understands the fundamentals well enough to tell when the output is wrong; the mechanics are right; and the result passes the same check without a hero.

290
00:22:59.378 --> 00:23:02.895
Scale the load to the size of the team and keep the movement.

291
00:23:02.895 --> 00:23:05.316
Plan the slack. Fitness improves the odds.

292
00:23:05.316 --> 00:23:06.930
It does not remove bad luck.

293
00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:12.374
A note on the borrowing. This chapter borrows CrossFit's models as a lens for companies.

294
00:23:12.374 --> 00:23:17.525
It is not an endorsement of any training program or diet, and it makes no medical claims.

295
00:23:17.525 --> 00:23:22.156
The health figures and the diet lines are Glassman's, quoted to show the models.

296
00:23:22.156 --> 00:23:24.645
Some of his health arguments are contested.

297
00:23:24.645 --> 00:23:29.970
This chapter takes no position on them, or on his wider scientific or medical controversies.

298
00:23:30.310 --> 00:23:32.805
CrossFit is a trademark of CrossFit, L.L.C.

299
00:23:32.805 --> 00:23:36.055
Neither this book nor Clark Huang is affiliated with it.

300
00:23:36.055 --> 00:23:41.685
The made-up subscription-billing company and its numbers are an illustration, not a real company.

301
00:23:41.685 --> 00:23:45.979
The belief about fundamentals and AI is my opinion, not a measured result.

302
00:23:45.979 --> 00:23:51.376
As everywhere on this site, these are opinions, not professional, legal, or financial advice.

303
00:23:51.376 --> 00:23:54.220
The sources, with links, are on the chapter page.

304
00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:57.403
And ask the question this chapter started with.

305
00:23:57.403 --> 00:24:02.646
Where are you using AI on top of fundamentals you understand, and where are you vibing?

306
00:24:02.646 --> 00:24:05.660
My belief is that the first is where the power is.

307
00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:08.647
Business Fitness in one hundred Words.

308
00:24:08.647 --> 00:24:14.869
Spend on customers and product, people and tools, a little on overhead, nothing on vanity.

309
00:24:14.869 --> 00:24:18.394
Keep cash at levels that fund growth but not waste.

310
00:24:18.394 --> 00:24:22.335
Practice the core movements: build, sell, serve, collect.

311
00:24:22.335 --> 00:24:27.796
Master the basics: clean data, clear owners, written decisions, honest numbers.

312
00:24:27.796 --> 00:24:30.631
Ship, test, learn and fix, hard and fast.

313
00:24:30.631 --> 00:24:35.193
Every week, mix these in as many combinations as the work demands.

314
00:24:35.193 --> 00:24:37.751
Routine is the enemy; process is not.

315
00:24:37.751 --> 00:24:39.963
Keep projects short and intense.

316
00:24:39.963 --> 00:24:42.867
Put AI in every movement, mechanics first.

317
00:24:42.867 --> 00:24:45.978
Regularly take on problems you've never seen.

318
00:24:45.978 --> 00:24:48.190
Measure it, or it didn't happen.

319
00:24:48.540 --> 00:24:51.620
Well is a reading. Fit is a margin.

320
00:24:51.620 --> 00:24:55.580
Silicon Valley C Players, chapter thirty-six.
